The '''34th Street–Herald Square station''' (also signed as '''34th Street''') is an underground [[metro station|station]] complex on the [[BMT Broadway Line]] and the [[IND Sixth Avenue Line]] of the [[New York City Subway]]. It is located at [[Herald Square]] in [[Midtown Manhattan]] where [[34th Street (Manhattan)|34th Street]], [[Broadway (Manhattan)|Broadway]] and [[Sixth Avenue (Manhattan)|Sixth Avenue]] (Avenue of the Americas) intersect, and is served by the [[D (New York City Subway service)|D]], [[F (New York City Subway service)|F]], [[N (New York City Subway service)|N]], and [[Q (New York City Subway service)|Q]] trains at all times; the [[R (New York City Subway service)|R]] train at all times except late nights; the [[B (New York City Subway service)|B]], [[M (New York City Subway service)|M]], and [[W (New York City Subway service)|W]] trains on weekdays; and the [[Fd (New York City Subway service)|&lt;F&gt;]] train during rush hours in the peak direction.

The Broadway Line platforms opened on January 5, 1918, as part of the Broadway Line, which was built for the [[Brooklyn–Manhattan Transit Corporation]] (BMT) as part of the [[Dual Contracts]]. The Sixth Avenue Line platforms opened in 1940, completing construction of the first phase of the [[Independent Subway System]] (IND). The IND platforms functioned as a terminal for some trains until 1968, when the [[Chrystie Street Connection]] opened. The station complex was renovated in the 1990s.

The BMT and IND stations both have two [[island platform]]s and four tracks, and there is a mezzanine above both sets of platforms. The station complex contains elevators, which make it compliant with the [[Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990]]. There is an out-of-system connection to the [[PATH (rail system)|PATH]]'s [[33rd Street station (PATH)|33rd Street station]], as well as closed passageways to the adjacent [[42nd Street–Bryant Park (IND Sixth Avenue Line)|42nd Street–Bryant Park]] station and to [[34th Street–Penn Station (IRT Broadway–Seventh Avenue Line)|34th Street–Penn Station]] on the [[IRT Broadway–Seventh Avenue Line]]. The 34th Street–Herald Square station is the third-busiest station in the system as of 2019, with over 39 million passengers entering the station that year.<ref name="2015-ridership">{{NYCS const|riderref}}</ref>

==History==
=== Dual Contracts ===
The [[New York Public Service Commission]] adopted plans for what was known as the Broadway–Lexington Avenue route (later the [[BMT Broadway Line|Broadway Line]]) on December 31, 1907.<ref name="Walker 1918">{{cite book|last1=Walker|first1=James Blaine|url=https://archive.org/details/fiftyyearsrapid00walkgoog|title=Fifty Years of Rapid Transit — 1864 to 1917|date=1918|publisher=Law Printing|location=New York, N.Y.|access-date=November 6, 2016}}</ref>{{Rp|212}} A proposed Tri-borough system was adopted in early 1908, incorporating the Broadway Line. Operation of the line was assigned to the [[Brooklyn Rapid Transit Company]] (BRT; after 1923, the [[Brooklyn–Manhattan Transit Corporation]] or BMT<ref name="New York (State). Transit Commission 1924 p. 501">{{cite book | title=State of New York Transit Commission Third Annual Report for the Calendar Year 1923| publisher=New York State Transit Commission | issue=v. 3 | year=1924 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lFxLAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA501 | page=501}}</ref>) in the [[Dual Contracts]], adopted on March 4, 1913.<ref name="Walker 1918"/>{{Rp|203–219}}<ref>Engineering News, [http://www.nycsubway.org/articles/triboro.html A New Subway Line for New York City] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120705042944/http://www.nycsubway.org/articles/triboro.html |date=July 5, 2012 }}, Volume 63, No. 10, March 10, 1910</ref> The Public Service Commission approved plans for the segment between 26th and 38th Streets, including the [[28th Street station (BMT Broadway Line)|28th Street]] and 34th Street stations, in April 1914.<ref name="nyt-1914-04-01">{{Cite news |date=April 1, 1914 |title=Ready for Subway to Greeley Square; Plans for Broadway Section, 26th to 38th Streets, Approved. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1914/04/01/archives/ready-for-subway-to-greeley-square-plans-for-broadway-section-26th.html |access-date=May 15, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="n124743448">{{Cite web |date=May 23, 1914 |title=Lines in Manhattan Progressing Rapidly |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-citizen-lines-in-manhattan/124743448/ |access-date=May 15, 2023 |work=The Brooklyn Citizen |page=14 |via=Newspapers.com}}</ref> The contract for that section went to the second-lowest bidder, the [[United States Realty and Construction Company]], as the lowest bidder was too inexperienced.<ref name="nyt-1914-05-23">{{Cite news |date=May 23, 1914 |title=Bids for Subways Under River Opened; Booth & Flinn and the O'Rourke Co. Offer to Bore Both Tubes for $12,461,000. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1914/05/23/archives/bids-for-subways-under-river-opened-booth-flinn-and-the-orourke-co.html |access-date=May 15, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> U.S. Realty began constructing the tunnel between 26th and 38th Streets in August 1914.<ref name="n124743583">{{Cite web |date=November 28, 1914 |title=Subway Progress |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-brooklyn-daily-eagle-subway-progress/124743583/ |access-date=May 15, 2023 |work=The Brooklyn Daily Eagle |page=22 |via=Newspapers.com}}</ref> A. W. King was hired to install finishes in the 34th Street station in July 1917.<ref name=nyt-1917-07-20>{{Cite news|date=July 20, 1917|title=Award Contract for Station Finish.|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1917/07/20/archives/award-contract-for-station-finish.html|access-date=May 17, 2023|issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

The Broadway Line opened between [[14th Street–Union Square station (BMT Broadway Line)|14th Street–Union Square]] and [[Canal Street station (BMT Broadway Line)|Canal Street]] in 1917; it originally only served local trains.<ref>{{cite news|date=September 4, 1917|title=Broadway Subway Opened To Coney By Special Train. Brooklynites Try New Manhattan Link From Canal St. to Union Square. Go Via Fourth Ave. Tube|newspaper=The Brooklyn Daily Eagle|url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/2708189/the_brooklyn_daily_eagle/|access-date=May 31, 2019}}</ref><ref name=nyt19170905>{{Cite news|date=September 5, 1917|title=Open First Section of Broadway Line; Train Carrying 1,000 Passengers Runs from Fourteenth Street to Coney Island|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1917/09/05/archives/open-first-section-of-broadway-line-train-carrying-1000-passengers.html|access-date=December 27, 2020|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=June 12, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612191916/https://www.nytimes.com/1917/09/05/archives/open-first-section-of-broadway-line-train-carrying-1000-passengers.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On January 5, 1918, the Broadway Line was extended north to [[Times Square–42nd Street (BMT Broadway Line)|Times Square–42nd Street]] and south to [[Rector Street (BMT Broadway Line)|Rector Street]], and express service started on the line.<ref>{{Cite news|date=January 6, 1918|title=Open New Subway To Times Square; Brooklyn Directly Connected with Wholesale and Shopping Districts of New York. Nickel Zone Is Extended. First Train in Broadway Tube Makes Run from Rector Street in 17 Minutes. Cost About $20,000,000 Rapid Transit from Downtown to Hotel and Theatre Sections Expected to Affect Surface Lines. Increases Five-Cent Zone. First Trip to Times Square. Benefits to Brooklyn.|newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1918/01/06/102652686.pdf|access-date=November 5, 2016|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="p575840136">{{cite news |date=January 6, 1918 |title=New Broadway Subway Opened From 42d to Rector Street: First Train Carries B. R. T. and City Officials, With Representatives of Civic Organizations—Line Then Turned Over to Public |page=17 |work=New-York Tribune |id={{ProQuest|575840136}}}}</ref> Before the 34th Street station had officially opened, ''Women's Wear Daily'' reported that the stop would be "one of the most important subway stations in the world", as it would lead directly to three busy crosstown streets (32nd, 33rd, and 34th Streets).<ref name="p1700017944">{{cite magazine |date=April 10, 1914 |title=Herald Square's New Express Station |magazine=Women's Wear Daily |volume=8 |issue=83 |pages=MS12 |id={{ProQuest|1700017944}}}}</ref>

=== IND expansion ===
New York City mayor [[John Francis Hylan]]'s original plans for the [[Independent Subway System]] (IND), proposed in 1922, included building over {{convert|100|mi}} of new lines and taking over nearly {{convert|100|mi}} of existing lines. The lines were designed to compete with the existing underground, surface, and elevated lines operated by the [[Interborough Rapid Transit Company]] (IRT) and [[Brooklyn–Manhattan Transit Corporation]] (BMT).<ref>{{cite news |date=August 4, 1923 |title=Two Subway Routes Adopted by City |page=9 |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1923/08/04/archives/two-subway-routes-adopted-by-city-estimate-board-accepts-wash.html |access-date=August 1, 2019 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=March 12, 1924 |title=Plans Now Ready to Start Subways |page=1 |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1924/03/12/archives/plans-now-ready-to-start-subways-commission-notifies-city-it-can.html |access-date=August 1, 2019 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The IND Sixth Avenue Line was designed to replace the elevated [[IRT Sixth Avenue Line]].<ref name="nytimes.com2">{{cite news |date=January 11, 1930 |title=Delaney For Razing Elevated Line Now; Work in 6th Av. Could Begin in Six Months if Condemnation Started at Once, He Says. Sees Cut In Subway Cost Eliminating Need for Underpinning Would Save $4,000,000 and Speed Construction, He Holds. |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1930/01/11/archives/delaney-for-razing-elevated-line-now-work-in-6th-av-could-begin-in.html |access-date=April 27, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> In 1924, the IND submitted its list of proposed subway routes to the [[New York City Board of Transportation]] (NYCBOT), which approved the program. One of the routes was a segment of tunnel from Fourth Street to 53rd Street.<ref>{{Cite news |date=March 21, 1925 |title=New Subway Routes in Hylan Program to Cost $186,046,000 – Board of Transportation Adopts 22.90 Miles of Additional Lines – Total Now $345,629,000 – But the Entire System Planned by Mayor Involves $700,000,000 – Description of Routes – Heaviest Expenditures Will Be Made on Tunnels – No Allowance for Equipment – New Subway Routes to Cost $186,046,000 |language=en-US |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1925/03/21/archives/new-subway-routes-in-hylan-program-to-cost-186046000-board-of.html |access-date=April 8, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Part of this stretch was already occupied by the [[Hudson & Manhattan Railroad]] (H&M)'s [[Uptown Hudson Tubes]].<ref>{{cite news |date=November 20, 1924 |title=6th Av. Subway Plan Hinges On Tubes' Use; City Must Reach Agreement With Hudson & Manhattan to Carry Out Project. |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1924/11/20/archives/6th-av-subway-plan-hinges-on-tubes-use-city-must-reach-agreement.html |access-date=April 26, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> As a result, negotiations between the city and the H&M continued for several years.<ref>{{cite news |date=August 1, 1929 |title=Final Contracts To Finish Subway Awarded By City; Include $20,000,000 for Cars, Equipment and Substations for Manhattan Line. Operation Set For 1931 Board of Transportation Moves to Rid Sixth Avenue of Trolley Tracks. Seeks To Buy Franchise Line Willing to Exchange It for Bus Permit—Negotiations Pushed to Extend Tube. |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1929/09/01/archives/final-contracts-to-finish-subway-awarded-by-city-include-20000000.html |access-date=April 26, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The IND and H&M finally came to an agreement in 1930. The city had decided to build the IND Sixth Avenue Line's local tracks around the pre-existing H&M tubes, and add express tracks for the IND underneath the H&M tubes at a later date.<ref name="nytimes.com2"/> The IND started advertising bids for the section of the Sixth Avenue Line between 43rd and 53rd Streets in April 1931.<ref>{{cite news |date=April 21, 1931 |title=The Sixth Avenue Subway. |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1931/04/21/archives/the-sixth-avenue-subway.html |access-date=April 28, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

In April 1935, engineers started planning in earnest for the Midtown portion of the Sixth Avenue Line.<ref>{{cite news |date=March 1, 1935 |title=Plans To Be Drawn For 6th Av. Subway; 80 Engineers and Draftsmen to Begin Work at Once on Detailed Specifications. |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1935/04/01/archives/plans-to-be-drawn-for-6th-av-subway-80-engineers-and-draftsmen-to.html |access-date=April 28, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The city government issued corporate stock to pay for the $53 million cost of the project, since the line was not eligible for federal [[Public Works Administration]] funds.<ref>{{Cite news |date=August 8, 1935 |title=6th Av. Tube Work to Be Begun Oct. 1; Plans Speeded on $12,000,000 Section From 49th to 39th St. – Public Hearing Set. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1935/08/08/archives/6th-av-tube-work-to-be-begun-oct-1-plans-speeded-on-12000000.html |access-date=July 10, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="p1221734015">{{cite news |date=October 8, 1935 |title=Aug. 23 Set for Hearing On 6th Av. Subway Bids: Proposals for 39th to 49th Sts. Link To Be Considered Then |page=2 |work=New York Herald Tribune |id={{ProQuest|1221734015}}}}</ref> The first contract, for the section between 40th and 47th Streets, was awarded to Rosoff-Brader Construction in October 1935.<ref>{{cite news |date=October 31, 1935 |title=Subway Award Goes To Rosoff Company; Sixth Av. Contract Is Let Under Specified Use of Excavated Rock Despite Lower Bid. |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1935/10/31/archives/subway-award-goes-to-rosoff-company-sixth-av-contract-is-let-under.html |access-date=April 28, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="p128702539">{{cite news |date=October 31, 1995 |title=$6,040,000 Contract For City Subway Awarded |page=10 |work=Wall Street Journal |issn=0099-9660 |id={{ProQuest|128702539}}}}</ref> Mayor [[Fiorello H. La Guardia]] broke ground for the Sixth Avenue subway at [[Bryant Park]] on March 23, 1936.<ref>{{Cite news |date=March 22, 1936 |title=Mayor to Start Work on Sixth Av. Subway; La Guardia to Use Pneumatic Drill at Ceremony at Noon Tomorrow in Bryant Park. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1936/03/22/archives/mayor-to-start-work-on-sixth-av-subway-la-guardia-to-use-pneumatic.html |access-date=July 10, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="p1222069808">{{cite news |date=March 24, 1936 |title=Mayor Starts Construction of 6th Av. Subway: Demands Razing of 'El' as He Breaks Ground at Bryant Park Entrance Appeals for Unification Warns Rate War Is Likely to Follow Failure of Plan |page=9 |work=New York Herald Tribune |id={{ProQuest|1222069808}}}}</ref> The Carleton Company was hired in September 1936 to construct the section from 27th to 33rd Streets,<ref>{{Cite news|date=September 23, 1936|title=Subway Job Awarded; Carleton Company Is Favored for Sixth Avenue Link.|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1936/09/23/archives/subway-job-awarded-carleton-company-is-favored-for-sixth-avenue.html|access-date=July 11, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> and Rosoff-Brader was hired that October to build the segment from 33rd to 40th Streets.<ref>{{Cite news|date=October 28, 1936|title=Wins 6th Av. Subway Job; Rosoff Concern Gets Contract for 40th-33d St. Section.|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1936/10/28/archives/wins-6th-av-subway-job-rosoff-concern-gets-contract-for-40th33d-st.html|access-date=July 11, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

The line was built as a four-track tunnel north of 33rd Street, but there were only two tracks south of that street. The work largely involved [[cut-and-cover]] excavations, although portions of the subway had to be tunneled through solid rock.<ref name="p1223317978">{{cite news |date=November 7, 1937 |title=The Tube Takes Shape in 6th Av.: Most of the Excavation Is Done, And, Considering, Done Quietly: Despite the Intricacies of Underground Utility Facilities and Overhead Elevated Line, There Are Fewer Inconveniences and Complaints on Tliis Project Than Construction Usually Brings |page=A3 |work=New York Herald Tribune |id={{ProQuest|1223317978}}}}</ref> The builders also had to avoid disrupting the Sixth Avenue elevated or the various utility lines on the avenue. The line had to be built below the H&M and BMT tunnels, but it also had to pass above the [[East River Tubes]] at 32nd and 33rd Streets.<ref name="nyt-1936-05-17">{{Cite news |last=Robbins |first=L. h |date=May 17, 1936 |title=With the Subway Surgeons; They Are Performing a Major Operation on Sixth Avenue, Where Formidable Obstacles Are Piled One on Top of the Other |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1936/05/17/archives/with-the-subway-surgeons-they-are-performing-a-major-operation-on.html |access-date=July 10, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="p1240403778">{{cite news |date=May 18, 1937 |title=Real Estate News and Market Transactions of New York City and Metropolitan Area: 6th Av. Subway, 5 Years to Go, Is Taking Shape Tube That Passes Over and Under Other Tunnels Runs Into Some Problems Mid-Town Sixth ... Subway Station s It Looks Today |page=37 |work=New York Herald Tribune |id={{ProQuest|1240403778}}}}</ref> Excavation work was conducted 24 hours a day, with most of the blasting work being conducted at night.<ref name="p1223317978" /><ref name="nyt-1935-05-05">{{Cite news |last=Harrington |first=John W. |date=May 5, 1935 |title=City Plans Its Costliest Subway; In a Short Two Miles Under Sixth Avenue the Engineers Will Encounter Many Intricate Problems |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1935/05/05/archives/city-plans-its-costliest-subway-in-a-short-two-miles-under-sixth.html |access-date=July 11, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Workers had to use small charges of dynamite to avoid damaging nearby buildings or the parallel [[Catskill Aqueduct]].<ref name="p1242866935">{{cite news |date=February 17, 1938 |title=6th Av. Subway To Allow for Wide Pavement: Inspection Reveals Plan to Enlarge Street Space When Elevated Comes Down Burrowing Under the Heart of Manhattan for Sixth Avenue's New Subway Line |page=21A |work=New York Herald Tribune |id={{ProQuest|1242866935}}}}</ref> The section north of 33rd Street had mostly been excavated by November 1937.<ref name="p1223317978" /> The H&M's old 33rd Street terminal closed on December 26, 1937,<ref>{{Cite news|date=December 27, 1937|title=33d St. Terminal Closed; Hudson & Manhattan to End at 29th St. for a Time|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1937/12/27/archives/33d-st-terminal-closed-hudson-manhattan-to-end-at-29th-st-for-a.html|access-date=July 11, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name=p1243610818>{{cite news |id={{ProQuest|1243610818}} |title=United Hospital Drive Gets Gift of $25,000 |date=December 27, 1937 |page=14 |work=New York Herald Tribune}}</ref> and a rebuilt terminal opened on September 24, 1939.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1939/09/24/archives/hudson-tube-opens-terminal-today-remodeled-33d-st-station-cost-city.html|title=Hudson Tube Opens Terminal Today – Remodeled 33d St. Station Cost City $800,000 as Part of 6th Ave. Subway Expense – Closed for Two Years – Two Train Platforms and 3 Sets of Tracks Among New Transit Equipment|date=September 24, 1939|work=The New York Times|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331|access-date=April 15, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180416073451/https://www.nytimes.com/1939/09/24/archives/hudson-tube-opens-terminal-today-remodeled-33d-st-station-cost-city.html|archive-date=April 16, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=p1319976772>{{cite news |id={{ProQuest|1319976772}} |title=Hudson & Manhattan Railway Opens New Midtown Station: 33d St. Station Of Tubes Opens To BriskTraffic Jersey Crowds the Hudson Terminal Connection on Way to the World's Fair |date=September 25, 1939 |page=13A |work=New York Herald Tribune}}</ref> The IRT's Sixth Avenue elevated closed in December 1938, just before the Sixth Avenue subway was completed.<ref>{{Cite news |date=December 5, 1938 |title=Gay Crowds On Last Ride As Sixth Ave. Elevated Ends 60-Year Existence; 350 Police On Duty But the Noisy Revelers Strip Cars in Hunt for Souvenirs Suit May Delay Razing Little Threat Seen to Plan, However-Jobless Workers to Press Their Protest Makes Only One Stop Entrances Are Boarded Up Final Trains Run On Elevated Line Police Guard Structure |language=en-US |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1938/12/05/archives/gay-crowds-on-last-ride-as-sixth-ave-elevated-ends-60year-existence.html |access-date=April 26, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> [[Otis Elevator]] received a contract to install 16 escalators at the station.<ref>{{Cite news|date=July 23, 1939|title=16 Escalators Planned In New Subway Station|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1939/07/23/archives/16-escalators-planned-in-new-subway-station.html|access-date=July 11, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

The IND's 34th Street–Herald Square station opened on December 15, 1940, as part of the opening of the [[IND Sixth Avenue Line]] from [[47th–50th Streets–Rockefeller Center (IND Sixth Avenue Line)|47th–50th Streets–Rockefeller Center]] to [[West Fourth Street–Washington Square (New York City Subway)|West Fourth Street]].<ref name="nyt19401215">{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1940/12/15/archives/new-subway-line-on-6th-ave-opens-at-midnight-fete-mayor-and-2000.html|title=New Subway Line on 6th Ave. Opens at Midnight Fete|date=December 15, 1940|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|page=1|access-date=October 7, 2011}}</ref> The opening of the Sixth Avenue Line relieved train traffic on the Eighth Avenue Line, which was used by all services except for the [[G (New York City Subway service)|G]] Brooklyn–Queens Crosstown service. The line was initially served by the rush-hour [[B (New York City Subway service)|BB]], the [[D (New York City Subway service)|D]], and the [[F (New York City Subway service)|F]]; the BB and D terminated at 34th Street.<ref>{{Cite news |date=December 15, 1940 |title=The New Subway Routes |language=en-US |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1940/12/15/archives/the-new-subway-routes.html |access-date=April 15, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=December 5, 1940 |title=6th Ave. Tube Adds Two New Services – Provides Express Facilities to Queens and Local Trains to Washington Heights – Subway Opens on Dec. 15 – Changes in Routings on Other Lines to Bring Faster Time and Less Congestion |language=en-US |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1940/12/05/archives/6th-ave-tube-adds-two-new-services-provides-express-facilities-to.html |access-date=April 15, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=December 2, 1939 |title=New Subway to Add 2 Need Services – Opening of 6th Ave. Line to Provide Uptown Local Route and More Queens Expresses |language=en-US |newspaper=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1939/12/02/archives/new-subway-to-add-2-needed-services-opening-of-6th-ave-line-to.html |access-date=April 15, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

=== 1950s to 1990s ===
[[File:34th_St-Herald_Sq_td_(2018-08-17)_03_-_IND.jpg|alt=A "D" train arriving on the Sixth Avenue Line express tracks|left|thumb|In 1967, the express tracks at the Sixth Avenue Line's 34th Street station were extended to [[West Fourth Street–Washington Square station|West Fourth Street–Washington Square]].]]
On April 19, 1961, ground was broken for a $22 million project to build two express tracks between the [[West Fourth Street (IND Sixth Avenue Line)|West Fourth Street]] and 34th Street–Herald Square stations.<ref name=":6">{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1961/04/19/archives/construction-of-new-ind-tunnel-for-6th-ave-line-begins-today.html|title=Construction of New IND Tunnel For 6th Ave. Line Begins Today; Express Tracks Deep Under Street to Run From 4th to 34th St. – 1964 Finish Set for $22,000,000 Job|last=Levey|first=Stanley|date=April 19, 1961|newspaper=The New York Times|access-date=April 28, 2018|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The express tracks were part of an $80 million subway improvement program that began with the reconstruction of the [[DeKalb Avenue (BMT Fourth Avenue Line)|DeKalb Avenue]] station in Brooklyn. The project was part of the [[Chrystie Street Connection]], which would allow 45 additional trains per hour, carrying a combined 90,000 passengers, to enter Manhattan during rush hours.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1961/04/19/101458878.html?pageNumber=41|title=Construction of New IND Tunnel For 6th Ave. Line Begins Today; Express Tracks Deep Under Street to Run From 4th to 34th St. – 1964 Finish Set for $22,000,000 Job|date=April 19, 1961|newspaper=The New York Times|access-date=July 8, 2016|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.thejoekorner.com/brochures/index-6av-exp-tracks.html|title=Ground Breaking Program For Additional Subway Tracks|date=April 19, 1961|publisher=New York City Transit Authority|via=thejoekorner.com|access-date=January 25, 2016}}</ref> On November 26, 1967, the first part of the Chrystie Street Connection opened and Sixth Avenue Line express tracks opened from 34th Street–Herald Square to West Fourth Street. With the opening of the connection to the Manhattan Bridge, BB service was renamed B and it was extended via the new express tracks and the connection to the [[BMT West End Line]] in Brooklyn. D service was routed via the connection and onto the [[BMT Brighton Line]] instead of via the Culver Line. It only ran express during rush hours. F service was extended from Broadway–Lafayette Street during rush hours, and from 34th Street during other times to Coney Island via the Culver Line.<ref name="Cite RNT">{{Cite Routes Not Taken}}</ref>{{Rp|216–217}}<ref name="NYTimes-ChrystieStChanges-1967">{{cite news|url=https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1967/11/16/90418352.pdf|title=Subway Changes to Speed Service: Major Alterations in Maps, Routes and Signs Will Take Effect Nov. 26|last1=Perlmutter|first1=Emanuel|date=November 16, 1967|newspaper=The New York Times|issn=0362-4331|access-date=July 7, 2015}}</ref><ref name="NYTimes-ChrystieSt-Bewilder-1967">{{cite news|url=https://query.nytimes.com/gst/abstract.html?res=9C0CE0DD1738E23ABC4F51DFB767838C679EDE|title=BMT-IND Changes Bewilder Many – Transit Authority Swamped With Calls From Riders as New System Starts|last1=Perlmutter|first1=Emanuel|date=November 27, 1967|newspaper=The New York Times|issn=0362-4331|access-date=August 23, 2015}}</ref>

On November 28, 1969, the turnstiles and exit gates at the northern end of the station were relocated, making four more staircases from the Broadway Line platforms available for transfers to the Sixth Avenue platforms. Previously, transfers could only be made from two staircases.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/24726178/daily_news/|title=Easier Transfer at 34th & 6th|date=November 28, 1969|work=New York Daily News|access-date=November 5, 2018}}</ref> This complex was overhauled in the late 1970s. The Transit Authority fixed the station's structure and renovated its appearance. The overhaul replaced the original wall tiles, old signs, and incandescent lighting to the 1970s modern look wall tile band and tablet mosaics, signs and fluorescent lights. It also fixed staircases and platform edges.

On February 9, 1983, the [[Metropolitan Transportation Authority]] (MTA) announced that it had awarded a $6.1 million contract to replace 16 escalators at the station to two companies.<ref>{{Cite news |last= |first= |date=February 10, 1983 |title=Escalator Contract Awarded |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1983/02/10/nyregion/escalator-contract-awarded.html |access-date=February 22, 2024 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

In the early 1990s, the station received another major repair, which included an upgrade for [[Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990|ADA-accessibility]] and modernized wall tiling. The MTA repaired the staircases, re-tiling for the walls, installed new tiling on the floors, upgraded the station's lights and the public address system, installing ADA safety treads along the platform edge, new signs, and new track-beds in both directions.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |url=https://www.flickr.com/photos/unionturnpike/48475773341/in/album-72157710146388362/ |title=February 1997 NYC Transit Committee Agenda |publisher=Metropolitan Transportation Authority |year=1997 |pages=75}}</ref> [[William Nicholas Bodouva & Associates]], the renovation architects, also intended to streamline various passageways and ramps that had been added piecemeal over the years.<ref name="nyt-1993-03-26">{{cite news |last=Muschamp |first=Herbert |date=March 26, 1993 |title=Critic's Notebook; Touring New York Underground |page=C1 |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1993/03/26/arts/critic-s-notebook-touring-new-york-underground.html |access-date=November 18, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The $66.4 million project was completed in 1996.<ref name=":0" />

=== Since 2000 ===
[[File:Herald_Sq_music_2_vc_(cropped).jpg|thumb|A music performance in the mezzanine]]
On August 28, 2004, [[Shahawar Matin Siraj]] and James Elshafay were arrested for planning to bomb the Herald Square station during the [[2004 Republican National Convention]]. Elshafay cooperated with prosecutors and received a plea deal; Siraj was convicted of conspiracy on four counts, the most serious of which was plotting to bomb a public transportation system, in 2006<ref>{{Cite news|last=Rashbaum|first=William K.|date=May 25, 2006|title=Guilty Verdict in Plot to Bomb Subway Station|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/05/25/nyregion/25herald.html|access-date=July 11, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> and was sentenced to 30 years in prison in 2007.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Rashbaum|first=William K.|date=January 9, 2007|title=Man Gets 30 Years in Subway Bomb Plot|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2007/01/09/nyregion/09plot.html|access-date=July 11, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

The station's token booths were shuttered in May 2005, after fare tokens were replaced with [[MetroCard]]s; station agents were deployed elsewhere in the station to answer passengers' queries. This was part of a pilot program that was tested at seven other stations.<ref name=nyt-2005-05-03>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2005/05/03/nyregion/eight-subway-stations-to-deploy-agents-to-assist-passengers.html|title=Eight Subway Stations to Deploy Agents to Assist Passengers|last=Chan|first=Sewell|date=May 3, 2005|newspaper=The New York Times|issn=0362-4331|access-date=August 19, 2016|archive-date=May 29, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150529171410/http://www.nytimes.com/2005/05/03/nyregion/eight-subway-stations-to-deploy-agents-to-assist-passengers.html|url-status=live}}</ref> To save energy, the MTA installed variable-speed escalators at 34th Street–Herald Square and three other subway stations in August 2008,<ref>{{Cite news|last=Chan|first=Sewell|date=August 6, 2008|title=M.T.A. Rolls Out Escalators With Conservation Features|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2008/08/06/nyregion/06escalators.html|access-date=October 10, 2022|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> although not all of the escalators initially functioned as intended.<ref>{{cite web | last=Chan | first=Sewell | title=Bumpy Start for 'Green' Subway Escalators | website=The New York Times | date=August 11, 2008 | url=https://archive.nytimes.com/cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2008/08/11/bumpy-start-for-green-subway-escalators/ | access-date=October 10, 2022}}</ref> A Korean restaurant, [[Noksu (restaurant)|Nōksu]], opened on the station's mezzanine level in late 2023.<ref name="Orlow 2023 w171">{{cite web | last=Orlow | first=Emma | title=A Fine Dining Restaurant Opens in a Midtown Subway Station | website=Eater NY | date=October 3, 2023 | url=https://ny.eater.com/2023/10/3/23901404/noksu-opening-subway-baekjeong-herald-square | access-date=October 6, 2023}}</ref><ref name="Rahmanan 2023 m025">{{cite web | last=Rahmanan | first=Anna | title=A new fine dining Korean restaurant is opening in a subway station | website=Time Out New York | date=October 4, 2023 | url=https://www.timeout.com/newyork/news/a-new-fine-dining-korean-restaurant-is-opening-in-a-subway-station-100423 | access-date=October 6, 2023}}</ref> In November 2024, the [[United States Department of Transportation]] provided a $72 million grant to cover two-thirds of the design and engineering phase of the renovation of [[New York Penn Station]]. Among other things, the project will include a direct connection from the 34th Street–Herald Square station to Penn Station.<ref>{{cite web |date=November 13, 2024 |title=Penn Station renovations: $72 million federal grant on way, Sen. Schumer's office says |url=https://www.newsday.com/long-island/transportation/penn-station-renovations-upgrades-k9nvdvmx |access-date=November 18, 2024 |website=Newsday}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Simko-Bednarski |first=Evan |date=November 12, 2024 |title=Federal Transportation Dept. set to kick in $72M toward Penn Station renovations: Sen. Schumer |url=https://www.nydailynews.com/2024/11/12/federal-transportation-dept-set-to-kick-in-72m-toward-penn-station-renovations-sen-schumer/ |access-date=November 18, 2024 |website=New York Daily News}}</ref>
{{Clear}}

==Station layout==
This station complex has a long [[mezzanine (architecture)|mezzanine]] above the platforms. Three staircases and two elevators lead to each of the two Broadway line platforms. Three pairs of escalators lead to the Sixth Avenue line platforms (two to the northbound one and one to the southbound one). There is a non-ADA-compliant ramp that leads to an intermediate level. This level has two sets of staircases leading to each of the Sixth Avenue platforms. The elevators to this level are at the north end of the mezzanine.

Two works of art are installed on the mezzanine as part of the [[MTA Arts & Design]] program.<ref name="Rubinstein 2012" /> The first of these is ''Radiant Site'' by [[Michele Oka Doner]], which consists of 11,000 gold-colored wall tiles.<ref name="nyt19901122">{{cite news |last=Louie |first=Elaine |date=November 22, 1990 |title=Currents; Where Art Is Blowin' In the Wind |page=C3 |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/11/22/garden/currents-where-art-is-blowin-in-the-wind.html |access-date=November 17, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="Carrigan 2018">{{cite web |last=Carrigan |first=Margaret |date=February 15, 2018 |title=Artist Who Gilded NYC's 34th Street Subway Station Takes on Detroit |url=https://observer.com/2018/02/michele-oka-doner-brings-her-elemental-art-to-wasserman-projects/ |access-date=November 18, 2022 |website=Observer}}</ref> The tiles were manufactured at [[Pewabic Pottery]] in Michigan<ref name="MTA Radiant Site">{{cite web |title=Radiant Site |url=https://new.mta.info/agency/arts-design/collection/radiant-site |access-date=November 18, 2022 |website=MTA}}</ref> and installed along a {{convert|165|ft|m|-long|adj=mid}} stretch of passageway;<ref name="Carrigan 2018" /> according to Oka Doner, they were meant "to fill the underground with light and feeling".<ref name="nyt19901122" /> The second artwork is ''Halo'' by Nicholas Pearson and consists of seven orbs,<ref name="nyt19901122" /> each made of coiled aluminum rods and placed on small beams above the mezzanine.<ref name="Rubinstein 2012" /><ref name="MTA Halo">{{cite web |title=Halo |url=https://new.mta.info/agency/arts-design/collection/halo |access-date=November 18, 2022 |website=MTA}}</ref> The MTA selected these artworks, along with David Provan's ''Yab-Yum'' sculpture above the IND platform, as part of the Herald Square Subway Complex Art Competition in 1988.<ref name="nyt19901122" /> [[Herbert Muschamp]] of ''[[The New York Times]]'' wrote of the artworks: "Unfortunately, though one feels like a subway vandal to say so, the plan's parts don't add up to an effective whole. A surfeit of mismatched design elements defeats the intended airiness."<ref name="nyt-1993-03-26" />

{{Midtown Manhattan subway cross section}}

===Exits===
At the north end of the primary mezzanine is the 35th Street exit, which contains a bank of turnstiles, token booth, and street stairs. Three staircases lead to all corners of Sixth Avenue and 35th Street except the southwest one. There is also a short passageway to either western corner of Broadway and 35th Street.<ref name=MTA-PennStation-2015>{{cite web|title=MTA Neighborhood Maps: Pennsylvania Station / Times Square|url=http://web.mta.info/maps/neighborhoods/mn/M08_PennStation_2015.pdf|website=[[Metropolitan Transportation Authority|mta.info]]|publisher=[[Metropolitan Transportation Authority]]|access-date=December 11, 2015|date=2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200914173833/http://web.mta.info/maps/neighborhoods/mn/M08_PennStation_2015.pdf|archive-date=September 14, 2020|url-status=dead}}</ref>

When the station was built, there were entrances at each of four corners of Broadway, Sixth Avenue, and 34th Street. Two stairs each led to the sidewalk outside [[Macy's Herald Square]] on the northwest corner; the [[Marbridge Building]] on the northeast corner; the [[Hotel McAlpin]] on the southeast corner; and Saks on the southwest corner.<ref name="p1700017944" /> {{As of|2023}}, the primary mezzanine has connections with the two entrances at Broadway/Sixth Avenue and 34th Street. The entrance on the west side is staffed full-time and has two staircases to 34th Street. The northwest staircase has an entrance to an underground [[Burger King]]. There is a long passageway containing a single street elevator that leads to [[Port Authority Trans-Hudson|PATH]] at [[33rd Street (PATH station)|33rd Street]]. The entrance on the east side of 34th Street is staffed part-time and when the token booth is closed, only two HEET turnstiles provide access to the mezzanine. This entrance has a passageway that connects to the 35th Street exit and has two pairs of exit-only turnstiles from the mezzanine.<ref name=MTA-PennStation-2015/>

Upon the station's opening, there were also four entrances at 32nd Street: three from the street and one from the PATH (then H&M) station.<ref name="p1700017944" /> As of 2023, there is a separate mezzanine at the south end of the Sixth Avenue level that has two staircases leading to each platform. It is directly underneath the PATH station mezzanine (two levels from street level) and has a passageway leading to the entrance at Broadway and 32nd Street. Outside of fare control, there is an entrance leading directly to the two basement levels of [[J. C. Penney]] in the [[100 West 33rd Street|Manhattan Mall]]. There are also escalators that lead to the front entrance of the mall. The entrance at Broadway and 32nd Street is unstaffed, has two street stairs, and one stair to each of the two Broadway platforms on the very south end. There are street stairs to either northern corner of Broadway and 32nd Street, as well as to the northeast corner of 6th Avenue and 32nd Street.<ref name=MTA-PennStation-2015/> {{As of|2023}}, the northeastern entrance at 32nd Street and Broadway contains the [[Noksu (restaurant)|Nōksu]] restaurant.<ref name="Orlow 2023 w171"/><ref name="Rahmanan 2023 m025"/>

===Passageways===
[[File:Ramp to BDFM at 34th Street Herald Square station, September 2018.JPG|right|thumb|Passageways in the station for transfers between the IND and BMT platforms]]
There are closed passageways (but not free transfers) to the adjacent [[42nd Street–Bryant Park (IND Sixth Avenue Line)|42nd Street–Bryant Park]] station to the north and to [[34th Street–Penn Station (IRT Broadway–Seventh Avenue Line)|34th Street–Penn Station]] on the [[IRT Broadway–Seventh Avenue Line]].

====Gimbels passageway====
There was once an out-of-system passageway under 33rd Street, which ran to [[Pennsylvania Station (New York City)|Pennsylvania Station]], one block west. The passageway was called the "Gimbels passageway" because it was next to the basements of the [[Gimbels]] department store and the [[Hotel Pennsylvania]].<ref name=p576220262>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/113543237/new-yorks-new-street-ties-together-trav/ |title=New York's New Street Ties Together Travel Routes of This City and Other Cities of Country |date=May 30, 1920 |page=A7 |work=New-York Tribune |access-date=November 23, 2022 |id={{ProQuest|576220262}}}}</ref><ref name=p1666189944/> The passageway was designed by [[McKim, Mead & White]] and constructed by the [[George A. Fuller Company]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=May 30, 1920 |title=Broadway-Pennsylvania Thoroughfare |pages=24 |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/113543174/broadway-pennsylvania-thoroughfare/ |access-date=November 23, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The Gimbels passageway opened on May 29, 1920, after one and a half years of construction.<ref name=p576220262/> It measured {{convert|800|ft}} long by {{convert|18|ft}} wide.<ref name=p1666189944>{{cite magazine |title=New Subterranean Thoroughfare To Gimbel Brothers' Opens Friday: New Subterranean Route To Gimbel's |volume=20 |issue=123 |date=May 26, 1920 |pages=40 |magazine=Women's Wear |id={{ProQuest|1666189944}}}}</ref> The south wall of the passageway originally contained 25 storefront windows, four entrances to the Gimbels store, and two entrances to the hotel. The north wall included 74 spaces for advertisements.<ref name=p576220262/>

Over the years, the entrances to the Gimbels store and the Hotel Pennsylvania were closed. During the 1970s and 1980s, the New York City Subway had high rates of crime, as did the rest of the city; the passageway similarly experienced high crime rates. This passageway was closed in 1986,<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=July 14, 2010 |title=City Planning Commission July 14, 2010 / Calendar No. 31 C 100049 ZSM |url=http://archive.citylaw.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/31/cpc/2010/07.14.10/C-100049-ZSM.pdf |archive-url= |archive-date= |access-date=March 3, 2019 |website=citylaw.org |publisher=City Planning Commission}}</ref> and passengers now must walk at street level to connect to the commuter railroads and Amtrak.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://nypost.com/2010/11/28/remembering-the-gimbels-tunnel/ |title=Remembering the Gimbels tunnel |work=New York Post |date=November 28, 2010 |access-date=December 14, 2018}}</ref> A real estate developer, [[Vornado Realty Trust]], proposed in 2010 to reopen the passageway as part of the development of the [[15 Penn Plaza]] office tower.<ref name="CityLand 2010">{{cite web | title=Commercial tower at 15 Penn Plaza approved | website=CityLand | date=August 15, 2010 | url=https://www.citylandnyc.org/commercial-tower-at-15-penn-plaza-approved/ | access-date=November 23, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Del Signore |first=John |date=February 4, 2009 |title=Underground Passageway Between Herald Square Subway and Penn Station May Reopen |url=http://gothamist.com/news/underground-passageway-between-herald-square-subway-and-penn-station-may-reopen |access-date=January 16, 2021 |website=Gothamist |language=en |archive-date=January 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210126070948/https://gothamist.com/news/underground-passageway-between-herald-square-subway-and-penn-station-may-reopen |url-status=dead}}</ref>

====Passageway to Bryant Park====
[[File:Old BMT and IND Subway Tile at 34th Street-Herald Square Station.jpg|thumb|left|Restored tile sign displays direction to former BMT and IND platforms.]]
The IND station's mezzanine originally extended north from 34th Street to the [[42nd Street–Bryant Park (IND Sixth Avenue Line)|42nd Street]] station.<ref name="nyt-1991-03-23">{{Cite news |last=Wolff |first=Craig |date=March 23, 1991 |title=Subway Path Boarded Shut After a Rape |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=http://www.nytimes.com/1991/03/23/nyregion/subway-path-boarded-shut-after-a-rape.html |access-date=February 1, 2018 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Proposed by the Board of Transportation in 1936,<ref>{{Cite news |date=June 18, 1936 |title=Long Walk Under 6th Av. Sought |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1936/06/18/archives/long-walk-under-6th-av-sought.html |access-date=July 14, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> the passageway was outside of fare control but was intended to relieve passenger flow at the 42nd and 34th Street stations.<ref name="nyt19401215" /><ref>{{cite web |last1=Kahn |first1=E. J. |last2=Ross |first2=Harold |date=May 4, 1940 |title=Underground |url=http://www.newyorker.com/archive/1940/05/04/1940_05_04_013_TNY_CARDS_000180572 |access-date=July 23, 2015 |publisher=New Yorker}}</ref><ref name="p1260972732">{{cite news |date=April 9, 1940 |title=8-Block Walk In New Subway Is Nearly Ready: Pedestrians' Underpass to Connect 34th and 42d St. Stations Under 6th Av |page=20 |work=New York Herald Tribune |id={{ProQuest|1260972732}}}}</ref> At both ends of the passageway, pedestrians could descend to turnstiles at platform level.<ref name="p1243069774">{{cite news |date=October 13, 1940 |title=6th Av. Subway Opens Dec. 15, A Month Early: $52,000,000 Job Praised by Mayor as 'Brilliant Engineering Achievement |page=1 |work=New York Herald Tribune |id={{ProQuest|1243069774}}}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=April 13, 1940 |title=Pedestrian Underpasses |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1940/04/13/archives/pedestrian-underpasses.html |access-date=July 14, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The passageway itself measured {{convert|35|ft}} wide and was very plain in design, with white-tiled walls.<ref name="p1260972732" /> There were entrances at 34th, 35th, 38th, and 40th Streets.<ref name="nyt19401215" /><ref name="p1260972732" /> At 35th Street, there was a smaller passageway extending westward to Broadway, near the northern end of the [[BMT Broadway Line]] station at 34th Street–Herald Square.<ref name="p1260972732" /> According to a 1940 report from the ''[[New York Herald Tribune]]'', the passageway was believed to be "the longest of its kind in the world".<ref name="p1243069774" />

In the 1980s, the passageway became a gathering spot for homeless people and drug users. On March 20, 1991, a woman was raped behind a pile of debris in the subway passageway during rush hour, which had entrances at 38th Street. Other commuters passed nearby but were unaware of what was happening. That passageway was closed the day after; it was used by 400 daily riders<ref>{{Cite news |last=Diamond |first=Randy |date=March 22, 1991 |title=Subway rape |page=12 |work=New York Daily News |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/28821041/daily_news/ |access-date=February 24, 2019}}</ref> and recorded 30 felonies since January 1, 1990.  In response, on March 28, 1991, the NYCTA ordered the closing of the 15 most dangerous passageways in the system within a week, which the [[New York City Transit Police|Transit Police]] and citizen advocacy groups had called for since the previous year. A woman was raped in the passageway in July 1990 with no response, but after another rape took place in August, the passageway's closure was called for by the local community board in September. Bureaucratic delays had prevented their closure, with their presentation to the MTA Board not scheduled until April 1991, after a public hearing on systemwide service reduction was to be held. The agency feared that closing the passageway without public comment would have caused an outcry for advocates for the homeless.<ref name="nyt-1991-03-23" /> The NYCTA's director of public information said that the agency had erred in waiting for formal approval. The locations were chosen based on crime volume, lighting, traffic and physical layout. These entrances were closed under the declaration of a public safety emergency, and were blocked off with plywood and fencing until public hearings were held and official permission was obtained.<ref>{{Cite news |date=March 30, 1991 |title=Tunnel vision |page=19 |work=New York Daily News |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/28821203/daily_news/ |access-date=February 24, 2019}}</ref>

