The '''34th Street–Herald Square station''' on the IND Sixth Avenue Line is an express station that has four tracks and two island platforms.<ref name=tracks/> The {{NYCS|D}} and {{NYCS|F}} stop here at all times,<ref>{{NYCS const|timetable|d}}</ref><ref name="ttf">{{NYCS const|timetable|f}}</ref> while the {{NYCS|B}} and {{NYCS|M}} stop here only on weekdays during the day.<ref>{{NYCS const|timetable|b}}</ref><ref>{{NYCS const|timetable|m}}</ref> The B and D run on the express tracks and the F and M run on the local tracks.<ref name=serviceguide>{{NYCS const|serviceguide}}</ref> The next stop to the north is [[42nd Street–Bryant Park/Fifth Avenue station#IND Sixth Avenue Line platforms|42nd Street–Bryant Park]] for all service, while the next stop to the south is [[23rd Street station (IND Sixth Avenue Line)|23rd Street]] for local trains and [[West Fourth Street–Washington Square station|West Fourth Street–Washington Square]] for express trains.<ref name=submap>{{NYCS const|map}}</ref>

The mezzanine elevators are at the north end of the station while the staircases to the Manhattan Mall entrance are at the south end. The platforms have numerous stairs and escalators leading to the main mezzanine. Stairs on both platforms lead to a non-accessible ramp leading to the mezzanine.

The platforms are not equal in length, as the northbound one is longer than the southbound one. North of this station are numerous crossovers and switches that allow trains from uptown to terminate here on the express tracks during construction and closures. The crossovers were reconfigured in 2018 to reduce the duplication of track switches of the downtown and uptown tracks, most notably, the switch from the express to local tracks.<ref name=tracks>{{NYCS const|trackref|trackbook}}</ref>

<!--Prior to the opening of the [[Chrystie Street Connection]] in November 1967, the express tracks only extended to [[bumper block]]s about {{Convert|140|feet|meters}} south of this station, though the tunnels extended for another {{Convert|260|feet|meters}} beyond that.
-->
Above the northbound local tracks is ''Yab-Yum'', a sculpture created by David Provan in 1992.<ref name="MTA Yab-Yum"/><ref name="Rubinstein 2012">{{cite web | last=Rubinstein | first=Dana | title=The pioneering subway art of Herald Square has seen better times | website=POLITICO | date=April 24, 2012 | url=https://www.politico.com/states/new-york/albany/story/2012/04/the-pioneering-subway-art-of-herald-square-has-seen-better-times-003540 | access-date=November 18, 2022}}</ref> It consists of fourteen paddles,<ref name="nyt19901122" /><ref name="MTA Yab-Yum" /> each measuring {{convert|12|ft|m}}, which are painted red to match the colors of the columns on the platform.<ref name="Rubinstein 2012"/> Whenever a train arrived or departed, it would create wind gusts that caused the paddles to spin.<ref name="MTA Yab-Yum">{{cite web | title=Yab-Yum | website=MTA | url=https://new.mta.info/agency/arts-design/collection/yab-yum | access-date=November 18, 2022}}</ref> By 2012, the propellers were no longer capable of spinning.<ref name="Rubinstein 2012"/>
